WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.600
Here's what a standard
account typically reads.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.896
Forest and domain topology, domain
controllers, sites, subnets and site links are

3
00:00:07.896 --> 00:00:13.112
readable, which gives you scope, functional
levels and the shape of replication.

4
00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:16.598
The OU hierarchy and Group
Policy links are readable:

5
00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:20.400
that's the structure the
attribute design later replaces.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.775
GPOs and their settings
reports are usually readable,

7
00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:27.225
unless security filtering
restricts a particular GPO.

8
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:31.350
Users, groups, computers and service
principal names are readable,

9
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:36.150
which is your identity inventory and
the start of service account discovery.

10
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.947
Certificate templates and enrollment
services sit in the configuration partition,

11
00:00:41.947 --> 00:00:45.050
so the AD CS dependency
surface is visible too.

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.313
AD-integrated DNS zones are
often readable; test it.

13
00:00:49.313 --> 00:00:52.154
What you usually can't
see is replication health

14
00:00:52.154 --> 00:00:54.712
and DNS server settings
such as scavenging.

15
00:00:54.712 --> 00:00:56.891
Those need a right or server access,

16
00:00:56.891 --> 00:01:01.249
so they become a delegation item or
an administrator-provided export.

17
00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:03.100
Why is the default so broad?

18
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.300
Read access is the sum of
several default grants.

19
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.752
Authenticated Users receives
the general, public,

20
00:01:09.752 --> 00:01:13.862
personal and web information
property sets on user objects.

21
00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:18.845
Pre–Windows 2000 Compatible Access
receives list, read-all-properties

22
00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:23.912
and read-permissions rights on users,
groups and InetOrgPerson objects.

23
00:01:23.912 --> 00:01:28.184
Its membership depends on a choice
made when the domain was created:

24
00:01:28.184 --> 00:01:32.456
in the Windows 2000-and-later mode
it contains Authenticated Users;

25
00:01:32.456 --> 00:01:35.812
in the older compatible
mode, Everyone and Anonymous.

26
00:01:35.812 --> 00:01:40.634
So in most domains a standard account
reads most attributes of users and groups,

27
00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:43.775
but the exact result depends
on the domain's history.

28
00:01:43.775 --> 00:01:46.657
The first step is therefore
a pre-flight that tests

29
00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:49.538
what this account can
actually read in this forest.

30
00:01:49.538 --> 00:01:53.090
Every later export records
which access it relied on.
